                    {
                        "sense_id": "3aed7cd3-734e-340d-bb82-10a1a8c84ddf",
                        "entry_id": "b6a002b4-ad7f-3380-aa0a-1de44f6f4c5f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "a trouvère, medieval lyric poet using the Northern langue d'oïl (precursor lects of modern French), as opposed to their older, southern example, the original troubadours, who used langue d'oc (Occitan)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "masculine"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2edfe3a3-f704-469c-8f23-e009a811d7d9",
                                "sense_id": "3aed7cd3-734e-340d-bb82-10a1a8c84ddf",
                                "text": "Ainsi, la poésie lyrique des troubadours et des trouvères, et surtout la portion de cette poésie qui roule sur les sentimens de galanterie chevaleresque, n'a pas une source latine; cette poésie est née avec la galanterie chevaleresque elle-même, […].",
                                "translation": "Thus, the lyric poetry of the troubadours and trouvères, and especially that part of this poetry which revolves around the sentiments of chivalric gallantry, does not have a Latin source; this poetry was born with chivalric gallantry itself, […] .",
                                "english": "Thus, the lyric poetry of the troubadours and trouvères, and especially that part of this poetry which revolves around the sentiments of chivalric gallantry, does not have a Latin source; this poetry was born with chivalric gallantry itself, […] ."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
